  "prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and resources available to House and Senate offices. The HouseNet website serves as a portal to restricted House offices only sites, including access to \"Dear Colleague\" letters, commercial database subscriptions, and assistance with archiving records, legislative drafting, and technical support. The House Legislative Resource Center offers current and historical House documents, directories, and access to various subscription databases. The House Parliamentarian provides assistance with legislative rules, precedents, and practices.\n\nThe Senate website and Webster site offer similar services for Senate offices, including access to commercial database subscriptions, legislative drafting assistance, and technical support. The Senate Library provides reference assistance and classes on research topics, while the Senate Parliamentarian offers assistance with legislative rules, precedents, and practices.\n\nCongress.gov is a public legislative website that provides timely, accurate, and complete legislative information, including legislation, floor votes, schedules, and the Congressional Record. It also offers access to a variety of government, commercial, and academic legislative sources.\n\nThe report also lists various resources available to congressional offices, including the Congressional Research Service, Congressional Budget Office, Government Accountability Office, Government Publishing Office, Law Library of Congress, and National Archives and Records Administration. These resources offer confidential, authoritative, and objective research and analysis on legislative and oversight issues, budget projections, independent reports on agency audits and program evaluations, and access to newly enacted laws and law numbers.\n\nOverall, the report outlines the various websites and resources available to House and Senate offices for legislative research, drafting, and technical support, as well as access to current and historical documents and publications.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides an overview of various websites and resources available to House and Senate offices. The HouseNet website serves as a portal to restricted House offices only sites, including access to \"Dear Colleague\" letters, commercial database subscriptions, and assistance with archiving records, legislative drafting, and technical support. The House Legislative Resource Center offers current and historical House documents, directories, and access to various subscription databases. The House Parliamentarian provides assistance with legislative rules, precedents, and practices.\n\nThe Senate website and Webster site offer similar services for Senate offices, including access to commercial database subscriptions, legislative drafting assistance, and technical support. The Senate Library provides reference assistance and classes on research topics, while the Senate Parliamentarian offers assistance with legislative rules, precedents, and practices.\n\nCongress.gov is a public legislative website that provides timely, accurate, and complete legislative information, including legislation, floor votes, schedules, and the Congressional Record. It also offers access to a variety of government, commercial, and academic legislative sources.\n\nThe report also lists various resources available to congressional offices, including the Congressional Research Service, Congressional Budget Office, Government Accountability Office, Government Publishing Office, Law Library of Congress, and National Archives and Records Administration. These resources offer confidential, authoritative, and objective research and analysis on legislative and oversight issues, budget projections, independent reports on agency audits and program evaluations, and access to newly enacted laws and law numbers.\n\nOverall, the report outlines the various websites and resources available to House and Senate offices for legislative research, drafting, and technical support, as well as access to current and historical documents and publications.",
  "score": 23.46938732637443,
  "answers": [
    "This report provides a brief list of key House, Senate, legislative support agencies, and executive branch offices, as well as links to online resources of use to new congressional staff who work with legislative procedures and conduct legislative research. Some of the websites listed are available only to congressional offices; other sites are restricted by chamber and are only available to those staff working in either House or Senate offices. This report is intended for congressional use only and will be updated annually."
